2026-08-24 — THE ALIGNMENT PROOF, AND A RESIDUAL OF A DEAD NUMBER.

form/reindex.py exists to prove ONE property: that a per-building attribute
computed in the mock can be attached to the shipped file BY INDEX. It proves it
by re-deriving the quantised height byte for every record and asserting it
equals the shipped stream element for element.

The published-height change broke that proof — 134 mismatches, exactly the 134
changed bytes, and `AssertionError: index alignment NOT proven`. Everything
downstream of it rests on that assertion.

THE REPLACEMENT IS STRONGER THAN WHAT IT REPLACES. "Ignore the mismatches"
would not be a proof at all, so the assertion is now SET INCLUSION AGAINST THE
FLAG COLUMN: the indices whose re-derived byte differs must be a SUBSET of the
indices the shipped `u` byte flags as FLAG_PUBHEIGHT. One unexplained byte in
either direction still fails. It checks the flag column against the height
column as well as the order. Measured: 134 differ, 153 flagged, and the 19
flagged that do NOT differ are buildings whose published height rounds to the
same byte as the LiDAR median.

AND A SECOND-ORDER DEFECT. roofs/roof-data.js ships `hf`, "the sub-metre height
the shipped file rounded away", so that a blockface published at 5.62, 5.94,
6.11 and 6.38 m does not draw as one bar. roofs/join.py took `h` from
reindex.build(), which re-derives it from the LiDAR — so for the 153 published
heights `hf` was the fractional part of a number that is no longer the height.
The same defect as the height itself, one decimal place down. 118 carried a
nonzero residual.

reindex.py gains build_shipped(), which carries the shipped height onto those
records, and roofs/join.py calls it. The published height is not recoverable
there — only the rounded byte is — so the residual for those records is a
DECLARED ZERO rather than a wrong fraction. At most 0.75 m on 118 of 166,166
roofs, stated rather than carried over. st is byte-identical; 87 packed hf
bytes move.

